// quarterpusher.comt -- THE QUARTER PUSHER
//
// Standing-over-the-machine view: a sloped table recedes away from you,
// narrowing toward a coin slot at the back.  Drop a quarter and it lands
// at the top of the slope; the table's own back-and-forth motion keeps
// the whole pile just barely unstable, so coins creep downhill a little
// at a time and, every so often, one tips over the near edge into the
// tray.  A fresh drop jolts the pile hardest right where it lands, so a
// new quarter at the top is often what sets off a slide that reaches the
// edge a few moments later.
//
// Start it like this:
//
//     comdraw -runfile src/comdraw/examples/quarterpusher.comt
//
// Then try:   pushhelp()
//
// HOW IT WORKS (the three layers, same as spirograph and etchasketch):
//   1. the MACHINE  -- pusher.pushstep() is one tick of the avalanche:
//                      every coin gets a small, oscillation-driven chance
//                      to slide downhill if nothing blocks it, and
//                      anything that reaches the edge falls into the tray
//   2. the DATA     -- each coin is an ellipse tagged kind="coin" with
//                      its own true lat (sideways position) and depth
//                      (how far down the slope), grouped with every
//                      other coin and the slot marker into pusher's
//                      own coinlayer; pusher.pushstep() reads
//                      $$(coinlayer) for them, the same stream-a-
//                      composite idiom orbit() and recipes() use on
//                      $$(frame()) in spirograph.comt
//   3. the MAGIC WORDS -- play() reads the keyboard (Shift+arrows, like
//                      etchasketch's keydrive()); demo() plays the
//                      machine by itself, since there's no real
//                      keyboard under xvfb to drive play() with
//
// pusher is one object (same bundling-data-and-behavior idiom as
// carousel.comt's carousel): held/traycount/phase/disturbance/slotlat/
// coinlayer are fields on pusher itself, and pushstep/drop/pickup/slide
// are dot-bound methods on it (play/demo/status/pushhelp are plain
// top-level funcs that call those methods from outside pusher -- see
// the note just below the object literal for why).  A self-bound call's
// frame IS the object's own permanent attrlist (see LANGUAGE.md's
// scope-chain section) -- a bare read/write of a field inside one of
// these methods is always live, no local()/global() wrapper needed,
// unlike a bare top-level func, which freezes a free variable's value
// at the func's own DECLARATION time and never re-reads it (see
// doc/LANGUAGE.md "Closures -- captured at declaration time").  Every
// other scratch variable inside a method -- loop counters, interpolation
// math, anything that isn't one of pusher's declared fields -- goes
// through temp(), exactly as carousel.comt's methods do: a self-bound
// call has no separate discardable frame of its own, so a bare write
// that ISN'T one of the object's declared fields would otherwise leak
// onto pusher as a permanent stray field instead of disappearing when
// the call returns.
//
// Every coin and the slot marker live inside one group, pusher's own
// coinlayer: the table's rocking motion is a SINGLE trans() on
// coinlayer each tick (see pushstep()), not a per-coin recompute, so
// the per-tick cost no longer scales with the pile size.  A coin's own
// position comes from trans(g sx,0,0,sy,tx,ty :set), the same
// absolute-placement technique carousel.comt's place() uses -- no
// move()-delta accumulation, so there's no sub-pixel rounding to fight
// -- but placecoin() is only called when a coin's own (lat,dep)
// actually changes (placed, slides, or falls), not every tick.  Per
// carousel.comt's own note: trans()'s six-value tuple must be bare
// variables or literal constants, never inline expressions or
// parenthesized sub-expressions, or it silently falls back to its getter
// instead of setting -- placecoin() below stages every value into a
// plain local first for exactly that reason.

// ============================================================
// the view: a trapezoid table receding from a near, wide edge (the
// cliff the coins fall off) to a far, narrow one (the slot)
// ============================================================

CENTERX=450
HW_NEAR=380; HW_FAR=100
Y_NEAR=140; Y_FAR=480
SCALE_NEAR=1.6; SCALE_FAR=0.4
R0=14
COINPAL="#C8C8C0","#D4D4CC","#BCBCB2"

// avalanche tuning: BASECHANCE is the ambient per-coin, per-tick slide
// chance from the table's own oscillation; dropping a quarter adds
// DISTURBBOOST to a shared disturbance level that decays by
// DISTURBDECAY every tick, so the slide chance (chancebase, in
// pushstep()) stays elevated for a few seconds after every drop.  A
// coin can only slide if nothing else sits within COINGAP of it, in a
// lane within LANEHALF sideways -- that's what makes a slide open up
// room for the coin behind it instead of every coin drifting at once.
BASECHANCE=0.0; DISTURBGAIN=0.1; DISTURBDECAY=0.85; DISTURBBOOST=1.3
MAXCHANCE=0.25
LANEHALF=0.14; COINGAP=0.035
SLIDEMIN=0.015; SLIDEMAX=0.05
PUSHSPEED=0.15; WOBBLEDEPTH=0.025
SLIDE_UI=0.06

// A literal-coordinate creation call (polygon()/rect()/line()/text())
// bakes in whatever pan the current view happens to have, while
// trans(g ... :set) (what every coin and slotmark use, via placecoin())
// always places a graphic's own origin at the given coordinates with
// no such offset -- so a backdrop graphic left as creation drew it
// lands in a different coordinate space than the coins, visibly
// misaligned.  trans(g 1,0,0,1,0,0 :set) right after creation strips
// that view-pan component back off, snapping the graphic to exactly
// its literal creation coordinates -- the same space placecoin() uses
// -- without needing to know what the pan offset actually was.
select(:clear)
table=polygon(CENTERX-HW_NEAR,Y_NEAR, CENTERX+HW_NEAR,Y_NEAR, CENTERX+HW_FAR,Y_FAR, CENTERX-HW_FAR,Y_FAR)
colorsrgb("#355238" "#355238")
pattern(2)
trans(table 1,0,0,1,0,0 :set)

// cliffline marks the housing's fixed edge -- it does NOT join
// coinlayer, so it stays put while the table surface (and the coins
// on it) oscillates past it, the same way a real pusher machine's
// cabinet edge doesn't move even though its table does.
select(:clear)
colorsrgb("#FFFF00" "#FFFF00")
brush(65535,5)
cliffline=line(CENTERX-HW_NEAR,Y_NEAR, CENTERX+HW_NEAR,Y_NEAR)
trans(cliffline 1,0,0,1,0,0 :set)

select(:clear)
brush(65535,0)
tray=rect(CENTERX-HW_NEAR-20,40, CENTERX+HW_NEAR+20,Y_NEAR-15)
colorsrgb("#303030" "#A0A0A0")
pattern(2)
trans(tray 1,0,0,1,0,0 :set)

select(:clear)
slottrack=rect(CENTERX-HW_FAR-15,Y_FAR+5, CENTERX+HW_FAR+15,Y_FAR+30)
colorsrgb("#303030" "#808080")
pattern(2)
trans(slottrack 1,0,0,1,0,0 :set)

select(:clear)
colorsrgb("#000000" "#FFFFFF")
title=text(CENTERX-180,Y_FAR+55 "QUARTER PUSHER")
trans(title 1,0,0,1,0,0 :set)

select(:clear)
brush(65535,0)
update()

// ============================================================
// LAYER 1+2: the machine
// ============================================================

// placecoin(g lat dep) -- set g's own position and size within its
// parent group from its true (lat,dep): the table's rocking motion is
// a separate, single transform on the coinlayer group (see pushstep()),
// not baked into each coin's own placement, so this only needs calling
// when a coin's own (lat,dep) actually changes.  dep runs 0 (the
// near/cliff edge) to 1 (the far/slot edge).  ("depth" is a registered
// command -- a bare assignment to it is a silent no-op.)
placecoin=func(
  g=arg(0); lat=arg(1); dep=arg(2);
  vd=dep;
  if(vd<0.0 :then vd=0.0);
  if(vd>1.0 :then vd=1.0);
  w=(R0*2)*(SCALE_NEAR+(SCALE_FAR-SCALE_NEAR)*vd);
  h=w;
  hw=HW_NEAR+(HW_FAR-HW_NEAR)*vd;
  cy=Y_NEAR+(Y_FAR-Y_NEAR)*vd;
  cx=CENTERX+lat*hw;
  pw=g.pw; ph=g.ph;
  sx=w/pw; sy=h/ph;
  tx=cx; ty=cy;
  trans(g sx,0,0,sy,tx,ty :set);
  nil)

// addcoin(lat dep) -- one quarter, with its own lane and depth
addcoin=func(
  lat=arg(0); dep=arg(1);
  select(:clear);
  g=ellipse(0,0, R0,R0);
  c=COINPAL@int(round(rand(0,size(COINPAL)-1)));
  colorsrgb(c "#60605A");
  brush(65535,1);
  pattern(2);
  g.kind="coin";
  b=mbr(g); g.pw=at(b 2)-at(b 0); g.ph=at(b 3)-at(b 1);
  g.lat=lat; g.dep=dep;
  select(:clear);
  colorsrgb("#000000" "#FFFFFF");
  brush(65535,0);
  placecoin(g lat dep);
  g)

// prefill(n [seed]) -- the table starts covered with quarters, like the
// real machine between games.  Appends to seed (an existing list) when
// given one, so the caller controls what comes before the coins in
// group() child order -- a group's children render back-to-front in
// list order, so whatever the table/backdrop graphic belongs behind
// must already be in the list before the coins are added to it.
// Returns the (possibly seeded) list, so the caller can fold it into
// the coinlayer group.
prefill=func(
  n=arg(0); seed=arg(1);
  if(n==nil :then n=45);
  created=seed;
  if(created==nil :then created=list());
  for(ppi=0 ppi<n ppi++
    created,addcoin(rand(0.0-0.8,0.8) rand(0.05,0.95)));
  update();
  created)

select(:clear)
slotmark=ellipse(0,0, R0,R0)
colorsrgb("#202020" "#FFFFFF")
brush(65535,1)
pattern(2)
slotmark.kind="slotmark"
smb=mbr(slotmark); slotmark.pw=at(smb 2)-at(smb 0); slotmark.ph=at(smb 3)-at(smb 1)
select(:clear)
colorsrgb("#000000" "#FFFFFF")
brush(65535,0)
placecoin(slotmark 0.0 1.0)

// ============================================================
// LAYER 3: pusher -- the machine's state and its magic words, one
// dot-bound object (see header for why its own fields need no
// local()/global() wrapper, and why its scratch variables need temp())
// ============================================================
pusher=(
  :held 0
  :traycount 8
  :phase 0.0
  :disturbance 0.0
  :slotlat 0.0
  :coinlayer nil

  // pushstep() -- one tick of the avalanche.  The table's rocking is
  // ONE transform on coinlayer (the group holding every coin plus
  // slotmark), not a per-coin recompute; disturbance decays toward the
  // ambient baseline; and each coin (checked against a snapshot of
  // everyone's position taken at the START of this tick, so one coin's
  // slide this tick never blocks or unblocks another until next tick)
  // gets a shot at sliding downhill if nothing's in its way -- only a
  // coin that actually slides gets its own placecoin() call, since a
  // coin that didn't move needs no new transform of its own.
  :pushstep func(
    temp(ph2)=phase+PUSHSPEED; phase=ph2;
    temp(wobble)=WOBBLEDEPTH*sin(phase);
    disturbance=disturbance*DISTURBDECAY;
    temp(ty)=wobble*(Y_FAR-Y_NEAR);
    trans(coinlayer 1,0,0,1,0,ty :set);

    temp(coins)=list(); temp(lats)=list(); temp(depths)=list();
    temp(cs)=$$(coinlayer);
    while(temp(g)=*cs
      if(g.kind=="coin" :then coins,g; lats,g.lat; depths,g.dep));
    temp(ncoin)=size(coins);

    temp(chancebase)=BASECHANCE+disturbance*DISTURBGAIN;
    if(chancebase>MAXCHANCE :then chancebase=MAXCHANCE);

    temp(gone)=list();
    for(temp(oi)=0 oi<ncoin oi++
      temp(mylat)=lats@oi; temp(mydepth)=depths@oi;
      temp(blocked)=0;
      for(temp(oj)=0 (oj<ncoin)&&(blocked==0) oj++
        if(oj!=oi :then
          temp(dl)=(lats@oj)-mylat; if(dl<0.0 :then dl=0.0-dl);
          temp(dd)=mydepth-(depths@oj);
          if((dl<LANEHALF)&&(dd>0.0)&&(dd<COINGAP) :then blocked=1)));
      temp(ng)=coins@oi;
      temp(slid)=0;
      if(blocked==0 :then
        temp(roll)=rand(0.0,1.0);
        if(roll<chancebase :then
          temp(slidestep)=rand(SLIDEMIN,SLIDEMAX);
          temp(nd)=mydepth-slidestep;
          if(nd<0.0 :then nd=0.0);
          ng.dep=nd;
          mydepth=nd;
          slid=1));
      if(mydepth<=0.0 :then gone,ng :else if(slid==1 :then placecoin(ng mylat mydepth))));

    // delete()-ing a coin while it's still a member of coinlayer leaves
    // the group's own view corrupted -- any later read of the group
    // (even just trans() or center()) then crashes.  trimgroup() first
    // removes it from the group cleanly (returning the group's new
    // identity, same as growgroup() does when adding), so the delete()
    // that follows is deleting a standalone graphic, not a grouped one.
    for(temp(gi)=0 gi<size(gone) gi++
      coinlayer=trimgroup(coinlayer gone@gi);
      delete(gone@gi);
      traycount=traycount+1);
    if(size(gone)>0 :then print("%d quarter(s) tipped over the edge!\n" size(gone)));
    nil)

  // drop() -- feed a quarter from your hand into the slot; the fresh
  // coin lands near the top of the slope and jolts the whole pile.
  // The new coin joins coinlayer so it rocks with the rest of the
  // table instead of needing its own per-tick transform.
  :drop func(
    if(held<=0 :then print("No quarter in hand -- pusher.pickup() to grab one from the tray!\n"));
    if(held<=0 :then return(nil));
    held=held-1;
    temp(jlat)=slotlat+rand(0.0-0.04,0.04);
    if(jlat<0.0-0.82 :then jlat=0.0-0.82);
    if(jlat>0.82 :then jlat=0.82);
    temp(newcoin)=addcoin(jlat 0.97);
    coinlayer=growgroup(coinlayer newcoin);
    disturbance=disturbance+DISTURBBOOST;
    update();
    print("Dropped! Holding %d.\n" held);
    held)

  // pickup() -- grab one back out of the tray
  :pickup func(
    if(traycount<=0 :then print("Tray is empty -- wait for one to fall!\n"));
    if(traycount<=0 :then return(nil));
    traycount=traycount-1;
    held=held+1;
    print("Picked up a quarter! Holding %d, %d left in tray.\n" held traycount);
    held)

  // slide(n) -- slide the drop slot n units left(-)/right(+)
  :slide func(
    temp(n)=arg(0);
    if(n==nil :then n=1);
    slotlat=slotlat+n*SLIDE_UI;
    if(slotlat<0.0-0.8 :then slotlat=0.0-0.8);
    if(slotlat>0.8 :then slotlat=0.8);
    placecoin(slotmark slotlat 1.0);
    update();
    slotlat)

)

// play()/demo()/status()/pushhelp() are plain top-level funcs, not
// pusher's own methods, and call pusher.pushstep()/drop()/pickup()/
// slide() from OUTSIDE pusher -- an ordinary dot-call, not a self-bound
// method calling its own sibling. That distinction matters here: a
// dot-bound method calling another method of the SAME object by name
// (`pusher.drop()` from inside another pusher method) intermittently
// fails ("not a func-valued attribute") the second time the identical
// call shape repeats with nothing else between the calls, and the same
// happens with the new selfdot form (`.drop()`) unless every call is
// followed by something that observably uses its result -- too fragile
// to build a driving loop on. Calling in from outside, as these do,
// doesn't hit it.

// play([n]) -- play for real: hold Shift (or turn Caps Lock on) and use
// the arrow keys, same shiftcapture trick as etchasketch's keydrive()
play=func(
  n=arg(0);
  if(n==nil :then n=3000);
  lastkey(:shiftcapture true);
  print("QUARTER PUSHER -- hold Shift or Caps Lock.  LEFT/RIGHT slide the slot,\n");
  print("UP drops a quarter, DOWN picks one up from the tray.  Esc stops.\n");
  done=0;
  for(fi=0 (fi<n)&&(done==0) fi++
    k=lastkey();
    while((k!=nil)&&(done==0)
      if((k=="Esc")||(k=="ESC") :then done=1);
      if(k=="LEFT" :then pusher.slide(0-1));
      if(k=="RIGHT" :then pusher.slide(1));
      if(k=="UP" :then pusher.drop());
      if(k=="DOWN" :then pusher.pickup());
      k=lastkey());
    pusher.pushstep();
    update(20000));
  lastkey(:shiftcapture false);
  print("Game paused.  play() to keep going, status() for counts.\n"))

// demo([n]) -- the machine plays itself: no keyboard needed, so this is
// the one to watch run (and the one that works headless under xvfb)
demo=func(
  n=arg(0);
  if(n==nil :then n=500);
  print("DEMO MODE -- the machine plays itself for %d ticks.\n" n);
  for(di=0 di<n di++
    if((pusher.held<=0)&&(pusher.traycount>0) :then pusher.pickup());
    if((pusher.held>0)&&(di%11==0) :then
      pusher.slide(int(round(rand(0.0-2.0,2.0))));
      pusher.drop());
    pusher.pushstep();
    update(15000));
  print("Demo done.  %d held, %d in tray.\n" pusher.held pusher.traycount))

// status() -- how many quarters are where
status=func(
  print("Held: %d   Tray: %d\n" pusher.held pusher.traycount);
  nil)

// pushhelp() -- remind me of the magic words
pushhelp=func(
  print("*** QUARTER PUSHER HELP ***\n");
  print("  play([n])        -- play for real: hold Shift/Caps, use arrows\n");
  print("                      LEFT/RIGHT slide the slot, UP drops, DOWN picks up\n");
  print("  demo([n])        -- watch the machine play itself, default 500 ticks\n");
  print("  pusher.drop()    -- feed a quarter from your hand into the slot\n");
  print("  pusher.pickup()  -- grab one back out of the tray\n");
  print("  pusher.slide(n)  -- slide the slot n units (negative = left)\n");
  print("  status()         -- how many quarters you're holding / in the tray\n"))

// ============================================================
// welcome!  (a pile of quarters already on the table, to start the game)
// ============================================================
srand(date())
// table goes into the list before the coins so it ends up at the back
// of coinlayer's render order -- a group's children draw back-to-front
// in list order, so table, listed last, would otherwise paint over
// every coin.
coinseed=list()
coinseed,table
coinseed=prefill(45 coinseed)
coinseed,slotmark
select(:clear)
select(coinseed)
pusher.coinlayer=group()
pusher.coinlayer.kind="coinlayer"
select(:clear)
print("+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n")
print("|               QUARTER PUSHER  --  a comdraw / comterp example                |\n")
print("+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n")
print("The table is already piled with quarters, shifting with every oscillation.\n")
print("You're holding 0, and 8 are waiting in the tray -- pick one up to start.\n")
print("Try: pushhelp()  pusher.pickup()  pusher.drop()  demo()  play()\n")
